Z||000007||WampusCat||11-21-2001||05:13 PM||||Saturn is a division of GM, like Pontiac, or Chevy.  For some reason, GM markets them as a "different kind of car company."  Made in Tennessee and Delaware.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Gold-86SE||11-21-2001||05:24 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by WampusCat:<BR><B>"A versatile seating package allows the Sky to convert easily from two-place to four-place seating. The extra seats in the rear stow away underneath the tonneau cover when not in use. <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>This is all I needed to know to understand why the concept car photo has such a seemingly long rear end.  Knowing this bit of info, I would never purchase this POS (Piece of Steel - Plastic compounds really).<P>This is as stupid as the Aztec, and sales would be poor.  From the outside it looks ok, really a plain jane for for a concept car.  However, I found the article interesting in the fact that Saturn was even considering, however remotely, a roadster.  Finally, in the classic since, I don't see how this is a "roadster."<P>It seems that General Motors will not ever have another two-seater outside of the vette.  Guess it's time for me to convert to an import guy.<BR>||||reg||1
