Z||000011||Nashco||11-21-2001||07:23 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fformula88:<BR><B>I saw a picture and some info on Saturn's website not too long ago.  I would almost start to get excited except that the writeup I saw sounded like it wasn't going to be much of a sports car and more of some kind of "Sport utility convertable" or something like that (my term).<P>It was also supposed to have its guages in the middle of the dash.  I ask, why does youthful have to equal guages in the middle of the dash??  (Read: Toyota Echo).  That alone would prevent me from buying it.  From a safety standpoint, I would also think it should be in front of the driver, not in front of the center armrest.  If they build one, maybe they will come to their senses!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>FYI, the first car that comes to my mind when center mounted guages are mentioned is NOT anything new.  The Mini Cooper came with center mounted guages, and the new version does as well.  I don't know how it could possibly be considered a safety hazard.  Any good driver doesn't have to STARE at the speedo to know how fast they're going.  I rarely look at my speedometer.  As long as I'm flowing well with traffic I'm fine.  If there aren't any cars, get to the desired speed and you can tell how fast you're going by throttle position, engine load, and exhaust noise.  I like center mounted guages, myself, cleans the dash up and balances the car in my opinion.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000012||AusFiero||11-21-2001||07:25 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Voytek:<BR><B>Good info.  If it ever comes to life, should be a LOT cheaper than all the Audi's and Beemers.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>It would want to be. I agree with the topless turd opinion. That things is dog ugly.<P><P>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/ausfiero4.gif">      <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A> | <A HREF="http://www.fieroshop.com.au" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Accessories</A>||202.139.20.3||reg||1
Z||000013||Fformula88||11-21-2001||08:39 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Nashco:<BR><B> FYI, the first car that comes to my mind when center mounted guages are mentioned is NOT anything new.  The Mini Cooper came with center mounted guages, and the new version does as well.  I don't know how it could possibly be considered a safety hazard.  Any good driver doesn't have to STARE at the speedo to know how fast they're going.  I rarely look at my speedometer.  As long as I'm flowing well with traffic I'm fine.  If there aren't any cars, get to the desired speed and you can tell how fast you're going by throttle position, engine load, and exhaust noise.  I like center mounted guages, myself, cleans the dash up and balances the car in my opinion.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I guess not everyone determines what speed they are going by the flow of the traffic or the sound of the engine/exhaust note.  Those funny dials that "clutter" up the dash are actually used by some of the motoring public.  I tend to use my speedometer a lot.  I monitor my speed to make sure I do not drive excessively fast because I find it very easy to be cruising along 5-10 MPH faster than I want to (or more!).<P>How can they be a safety hazard in the middle?  Same reason fiddling with the stereo or climate controls could be.  The farther away from where you need to look while driving, the longer your not watching the road.  Therefore, a center mounted Speedo is going to take your eyes off the road longer than one right in front of you.  Of course, were probably talking a mere second, or less of time but that could be the difference between stopping in time and looking for a good body shop.||||reg||1
